  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Friday)\nIssued at 108 PM CDT SAT OCT 3 2026\n\nThe overall synoptic setup and forecast expectations through the\nweekend remain largely unchanged. Key features include a stalled\nboundary to our north and a shortwave advancing inland from the\nGulf. Within 24 hours of onset, model uncertainty persists\nregarding the exact intensity of the Gulf system and the final\nalignment of the stationary front. Recent guidance trends suggest\nthe front has stalled farther north than previously projected,\nwhereas the shortwave appears slightly better organized as it\nmoves inland. Guidance continues to underrepresent the coverage\nof diurnally driven and outflow-dominant convection within this\nmoist, high precipitable water (PWAT) environment.\n\nThe heaviest rainfall remains projected for Sunday into Sunday\nnight as the cold front begins pushing southward across the\nforecast area. The highest totals are expected south of I-20,\nwith peak accumulations concentrated along and near the I-\n85 corridor closer to the track of the Gulf low. Widespread\nrainfall between I-20 and I-85 should range from 1 to 2 inches,\nwhile areas along and south of I-85 could see 2 to 3 inches. A\nfew deterministic models continue to hint at localized pockets\nexceeding 5 inches; while the high PWAT airmass supports these\nefficient rainfall rates, run-to-run inconsistency limits\nconfidence regarding exact placement.\n\nBy Tuesday morning, a deepening upper-level trough traversing the\nMidwest will drive the cold front fully through the region. This\ntransition will usher in a significantly cooler, more seasonable\nairmass for next week, bringing afternoon high temperatures down\ninto the upper 70s and lower 80s.\n\n16",
